<h2 id="section-synthesis">Synthesis Summary</h2>

### Top Findings

EP legislative pipeline for Q2 2026 focuses on three intersecting dossiers: AI Act delegated acts, defence industry fund disbursement, and CBAM implementation phase-in.

### Quarterly Outlook

The next 90 days (≈ 3 plenary sessions) carry elevated procedural density. EPP and S&D maintain coalition stability on flagship files while Renew introduces swing-vote pressure on digital provisions.

### Risk Surfaces

- **Trade defence trigger risk**: Medium — US tariff escalation could activate emergency trade instruments.
- **Coalition fragility on AI Act**: Low — EPP/S&D/Renew centrist block holds on delegated-act methodology.
- **CBAM phase-in timeline**: Low — Council and EP alignment confirmed for Q2 start.

### Cross-References

Supported by forward-projection artifact for 90-day horizon analysis.

<h2 id="section-supplementary-intelligence">Supplementary Intelligence</h2>

### Forward Projection

### 90-Day Scenario Map

**Baseline scenario (60 %):** EP legislative calendar proceeds as scheduled. AI Act delegated acts progress through committee scrutiny. CBAM implementation begins Q2 with minor technical delays. Coalition stability maintained.

**Upside scenario (25 %):** Trilogue acceleration on Defence Fund delivers political agreement ahead of schedule, boosting EP legislative output metrics. US tariff de-escalation removes external risk trigger.

**Downside scenario (15 %):** Trade defence emergency procedure disrupts legislative calendar. Commission Work Programme reprioritisation delays three flagship dossiers by 6–8 weeks.

### Forward Indicators

- EP plenary sessions: 3 scheduled for Q2 (April, May, June Strasbourg weeks).
- Committee priority dossiers: AI Act implementation (IMCO/LIBE), Defence Fund (AFET/BUDG), CBAM secondary legislation (ENVI/INTA).
- Council Presidency: Polish Presidency closes Q2; Danish Presidency assumes H2 2026.

